hey everybody jesse here and today we're  going to be breaking down everything  that you need to know about the latest  game from tango gameworks ghostwire  tokyo ghostware looks to be as if doctor  strange met dying light or control and  i'm honestly really excited to see what  it can bring to the table with its  unique style and look so let's dive in  [Music]  ghostwire tokyo is a new ip from the  evil within studio tango gameworks  however unlike the third person survival  horror of those games ghostwire tokyo is  something we rarely see a japanese  developed first-person shooter the game  places you into the shoes of a young  student in tokyo named atiku izuki who  wakes up after being hit by a car and  dying so we're starting off all yu  hakushon with this story because  afterwards izuku is saved by kk not the  singing dog from animal crossing but a  mysterious spirit who merges with izuki  allah shadow of mordor giving izuki new  supernatural abilities and this  ultimately proves to be incredibly  helpful given that nearly all of tokyo's  residents have mysteriously disappeared  and dark spirits now roam the streets  looking to take down anyone who might  still be left izuki begins to explore  the transformed city streets in order to  save his kidnapped sister from a strange  man wearing a hinaya mask for which kk  also wants revenge against for some  unknown reasons putting our two heroes  goals into alignment the two set out to  uncover the mysteries surrounding the  happenings in tokyo as well as learn  more about kk's mysterious backstory and  the strange figure in the mask after a  short opening segment the game allows  you to explore the open world  environment of the japanese city now  filled with monsters straight out of  japanese horror or scp-style stories you  got your slendermans your headless  schoolgirls your whatever the hell this  thing is the tokyo of ghostwire seems to  be beautifully rendered as well and  grounds you in its more otherworldly  elements as you set out to rescue your  sister however what's most important  about this game's world is that you can  feed the doggies walking around the city  already it's the best game ever like  many open world games you'll be able to  explore the city while trying to avoid  the mysterious creatures however to  unlock new areas of the map you'll have  to cleanse the spirits from tory gates  surrounding the city which will allow  you to continue your exploration while  you'll be able to head straight through  the game's story much of the exploration  of the game's world will come through  side quests with some of the more  friendly creepy new denizens of tokyo  asking for your help with various  problems one mission for example will  have you find a personal item for a  spirit in order to help them pass on to  the next life while our gameplay preview  writer richard wakeling did find some of  these side quests to be lacking in his  initial few hours of the game he did  also find some of the ways the game  explored japanese folklore to be  intriguing even further you can stock up  on your items at convenience stores run  by talking kitties again 10 out of 10 no  notes dotted around the map and you'll  also be able to find different buffs and  collectibles as you continue to explore  so all in all this is fairly standard  ubisoft style open world design albeit  with a japanese horror flair  similarly though physical movement in  ghost wire tokyo draws comparison to  another open world horror game that was  released earlier this year dying light  too indeed the tango ability will allow  izuki to swing around buildings  throughout the map by grappling to  flying monsters which will also be  paired with the game's gliding mechanic  however the game hopes to not only stand  out through its unique world but in its  combat as well after the game's  announcement publisher bethesda stated  quote the combat team at tango have  created an intricate combo-based system  that utilizes movements inspired by  traditional kujikiri hand gestures the  combat was in part crafted by sincharo  hari who was one of the driving forces  on doom 2016's excellent almost  rhythm-like violent gameplay setting a  high bar and legacy for ghostwire tokyo  to follow up on despite being modeled  after first-person shooters though uzuki  doesn't use guns but instead wields his  newfound super of natural abilities  through ethereal weaving which he  channels through various finger  movements in a way stylistically akin to  doctor strange like a sort of  supernatural avatar the last airbender  weaving allows you to channel different  elements like wind fire or water wind  weaving feels more defensive allowing  you to push enemies away and knock them  around but also charge it up for  potential instant kills fire is much  more damaging allowing you to shoot  explosive balls of damage towards your  enemies but is much more costly and how  much ammunition it takes up water  meanwhile works best for close quarters  combat allowing you to hit enemies  quickly and hard the game also has  defensive abilities allowing you to  negate damage and even parry to prevent  any damage done to you the developers  themselves equated this combat system to  karate meeting magic mixing movement  with style additionally your enemies  themselves will allow for a lot of  variety in gameplay certain enemies will  be more vulnerable to specific types of  damage will also expose their hearts  when attacked in the right way allowing  you to pull spiritual strings in order  to instantly take them down the combat  also features a skill tree that allows  you to unlock more abilities down the  line with many gameplay previews stating  that the combat really begins to unfold  and evolve once you begin to broaden  your skill set however this also means  that the initial hours of the game may  feel more limited as you're left with  fewer tools to handle your enemies  beyond the gameplay though as of right  now there has been no multiplayer or dlc  elements announced for the game meaning  that you're going to have to face the  horrors of tokyo all on your own  foreign as previously mentioned  ghostwire tokyo is being created by  tango gameworks a studio founded in 2010  by sinji mikami bakami is a towering  figure in video game history especially  within the horror genre he was the  director of the original resident evil  game and helped guide the series  development eventually directing and  helping wright the seminal resident evil  4. however after leaving capcom and  founding tango gameworks he eventually  created the evil within franchise that  seems to be more of a spiritual  successor to his work on resident evil  mixing third person survival horror  elements with a more surreal style story  and while ghostware tokyo seems to have  shifted gameplay genre tango gameworks  appears to be continuing to evolve its  style into the more ethereal less  grounded elements of horror within the  game while the evil within games  themselves are often considered to be  cult classics loved by devoted groups of  fans but never really reaching  mainstream appreciation we hope that the  new risks that ghostwire tokyo takes  with the tango gameworks formula will  help it reach a wider audience ghostwire  tokyo is set to launch on march 25th on  both playstation 5 and pc however the  game is supposedly a timed console  exclusive so xbox players will have to  wait a bit longer to jump into the  supernatural horror of tokyo streets  and that's all the basics you need to  know for the upcoming ghostwire tokyo  while it may be a big risk for the  studio it's exciting to see a new ip  trying to burst its way onto the  landscape especially one with such a  unique world and gameplay that will  hopefully pay off in the long run also i  just kind of want to buy it because you  can bet the dogs so you know i i have my  priorities straight if you want to know  more about ghostwire tokyo check out our  recently released preview of the game's  first two chapters so that you can be as  prepared as possible when the game  releases until then don't forget to  subscribe to the channel so you don't  miss all our coverage and forthcoming  review of ghostwire tokyo and everything  else we're doing here at gamespot  you
 

